= Solution
{parent=1}

<Spontaneous symmetry breaking> occurs when the <statistical Hamiltonian> is invariant under a <symmetry (physics)> but a chosen equilibrium pure state is not. A small selecting field or a <boundary condition> chooses one of several states related by the <symmetry (physics)>. For a finite unconstrained system the symmetric ensemble generally has zero <order parameter>; spontaneous order is defined by taking the <thermodynamic limit> before removing the selecting field. A ferromagnet with a selected magnetization direction is the standard example.

If the broken <symmetry (physics)> is continuous, nearby symmetry-related states cost no energy for a uniform displacement along the manifold of states. Locality and a nonzero stiffness then make a slowly varying displacement cost <gradient> energy rather than a mass term. These gapless long-wavelength fluctuations are <Goldstone modes>. In the conventional relativistic internal-symmetry setting their count is the number of broken generators. Nonrelativistic dynamics can pair broken generators, as in a ferromagnet; gauge <symmetries> and broken space-time <symmetries> require additional qualifications. A discrete broken <symmetry (physics)> alone does not produce a <Goldstone mode>.

For an interface with a selected mean height, the continuous displacement is a rigid translation normal to the mean plane. The tension gives a stiffness for spatially varying translations. The following calculation concerns this static Goldstone sector. It determines the excitation energy and equilibrium fluctuations, but a frequency dispersion requires a separate inertial or dissipative dynamics. In low dimensions thermal roughness also limits the existence of a strictly localized flat interface in an infinite system.

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $D=d-1$ for the membrane's internal dimension and assume $\sigma>0$. The small-slope expansion is
$$
\sqrt{1+|\nabla h|^2}=1+\tfrac12|\nabla h|^2+O(|\nabla h|^4).
$$
The zeroth-order term is the projected area and is independent of the height field. Discarding it changes only an overall normalization of the <partition function>. Thus the leading fluctuation energy and its statistical weight are
$$
H_2[h]=\frac\sigma2\int d^Dx\,|\nabla h|^2,\qquad
\boxed{\mathcal Z_2=\int\mathcal Dh\,\exp\left[-\frac{\beta\sigma}{2}\int d^Dx\,|\nabla h|^2\right].}
$$
One obtains this <Gaussian field theory> by discretizing the height field on a microscopic mesh, integrating every height with its Boltzmann weight, and then writing the continuum measure. A microscopic cutoff is retained for quantities dominated by short wavelengths. Quadratic order here really means small slopes: a large rigid height shift is still free because the energy has no dependence on $h$ itself.

The uniform height mode has zero energy. Fix its mean, impose a <boundary condition>, or divide out the integral over global translations to normalize the <partition function>. Height differences are independent of that choice. The approximation is the tension-only part of <small-slope elastic membrane energy>; there is no bending rigidity or confining potential in this part.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
h(\mathbf x)=\int_{\mathbf q}h_{\mathbf q}e^{i\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf x},\qquad
\int_{\mathbf q}=\int\frac{d^Dq}{(2\pi)^D},\qquad h_{-\mathbf q}=h_{\mathbf q}^*.
$$
<Parseval's identity> and $\nabla\mapsto i\mathbf q$ give the diagonal quadratic form
$$
\boxed{\beta H_2=\frac{\beta\sigma}{2}\int_{\mathbf q}q^2h_{\mathbf q}h_{-\mathbf q}.}
$$
In a finite periodic box one can equivalently use orthonormal discrete <Fourier modes>, for which each independent real amplitude has a positive stiffness $\sigma q^2$. Fourier orthogonality eliminates all cross terms between different modes. The zero mode is removed as explained in part (a).

The stiffness vanishes as $q\to0$, so <capillary waves> at long wavelength have arbitrarily low energy. The <statistical Hamiltonian> is invariant under $h(\mathbf x)\mapsto h(\mathbf x)+a$ for any constant $a$. Selecting a particular mean membrane height breaks this continuous normal-translation <symmetry (physics)>. A varying local translation therefore has a <gradient> cost but no restoring mass: these are <Goldstone modes>. The diagonal static kernel is $q^2$; it should not be identified with a dynamical frequency without specifying the surrounding fluid or membrane inertia.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Each nonzero mode is Gaussian, giving the <covariance>
$$
\langle h_{\mathbf q}h_{\mathbf q'}\rangle=\frac{k_BT}{\sigma q^2}(2\pi)^D\delta^{(D)}(\mathbf q+\mathbf q').
$$
Consequently the <capillary height-difference correlation> is
$$
\boxed{B(r)=\langle[h(\mathbf r)-h(0)]^2\rangle
=\frac{2k_BT}{\sigma}\int_{\mathbf q}\frac{1-\cos(\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf r)}{q^2}.}
$$
The difference removes the zero mode and makes the integral infrared-finite at each fixed separation. Where required, cut off $q$ at $\Lambda\sim1/a$ with microscopic length $a$. The question's $d$ is the embedding dimension, so the momentum integral has dimension $D=d-1$, not $d$.

For $d=2$, $D=1$, use $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dq\,(1-\cos qr)/(2\pi q^2)=|r|/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{B(r)=\frac{k_BT}{\sigma}|r|\quad(d=2).}
$$
The root-mean-square relative height grows as $r^{1/2}$: the interface wanders increasingly far from a fixed height.

For $d=3$, $D=2$, angular integration gives
$$
B(r)=\frac{k_BT}{\pi\sigma}\int_0^\Lambda\frac{dq}{q}[1-J_0(qr)]
=\frac{k_BT}{\pi\sigma}\log(r/a)+O(k_BT/\sigma),\qquad r\gg a.
$$
The <Bessel function> expression separates the universal logarithmic growth from a cutoff-dependent constant. The membrane is logarithmically rough, so its absolute height fluctuations are unbounded in the <thermodynamic limit> even though long-wavelength slopes need not be large.

For $d=4$, $D=3$, the massless Green function at nonzero separation is $1/(4\pi r)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{B(r)=B_\infty-\frac{k_BT}{2\pi\sigma r}+o(r^{-1})\quad(d=4),}
$$
where $B_\infty$ is finite once the microscopic cutoff is supplied. For a spherical sharp cutoff, $B_\infty=k_BT\Lambda/(\pi^2\sigma)$. Relative height fluctuations saturate at large distance, allowing a localized interface height selected by boundaries. **The roughness distinction is linear growth in $d=2$, logarithmic growth in $d=3$, and bounded long-distance fluctuations in $d=4$.** The short-distance cutoff and the small-slope assumption remain necessary; bounded height fluctuations do not imply a perfectly flat microscopic membrane.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Assume the pinning coefficient $t>0$. Here $t$ belongs to an actual energy, so its Boltzmann coefficient is $\beta t$. The quadratic kernel and <covariance> become
$$
H_2+V=\frac12\int_{\mathbf q}(\sigma q^2+t)|h_{\mathbf q}|^2,\qquad
\langle h_{\mathbf q}h_{\mathbf q'}\rangle=\frac{k_BT}{\sigma q^2+t}(2\pi)^D\delta^{(D)}(\mathbf q+\mathbf q').
$$
Therefore the <pinned capillary-wave correlation> is
$$
\boxed{B_t(r)=\frac{2k_BT}{\sigma}\int_{\mathbf q}\frac{1-\cos(\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf r)}{q^2+\xi^{-2}},\qquad \xi=\sqrt{\sigma/t}.}
$$
The normal-translation <symmetry (physics)> is now explicitly broken, and the formerly massless <Goldstone mode> has a restoring term. For $r\ll\xi$, but beyond the microscopic cutoff, the unpinned behavior is recovered. For $r\gg\xi$, the connected height <covariance> decays and $B_t$ approaches $2\langle h^2\rangle$, so roughness no longer grows without bound even for $d=2,3$.

In internal dimension one the exact continuum answer is
$$
B_t(r)=\frac{k_BT\xi}{\sigma}(1-e^{-|r|/\xi}).
$$
In internal dimension two the connected <covariance> at $r>0$ is $k_BT K_0(r/\xi)/(2\pi\sigma)$, where $K_0$ is a <modified Bessel function>; its zero-separation value needs the cutoff and the saturation value grows as $(k_BT/\pi\sigma)\log(\xi/a)$ for $\xi\gg a$. In internal dimension three the connected <covariance> is approximately $k_BT e^{-r/\xi}/(4\pi\sigma r)$ beyond the cutoff. Thus pinning replaces the long-distance massless tails by exponential decay. A negative $t$ would destabilize the quadratic <statistical Hamiltonian> rather than bind the membrane.

= 2
{parent=Paper 69}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <renormalization group> compares descriptions at successively longer length scales. First eliminate short-wavelength degrees of freedom, then rescale coordinates and fields to restore the reference cutoff. This changes the couplings, including interactions and additive free-energy terms generated during elimination. A <renormalization-group fixed point> is a scale-invariant description. Linearized directions growing under coarse graining are relevant, shrinking directions are irrelevant, and zero-eigenvalue directions are marginal and require higher-order analysis.

A diverging <correlation length> means that many coarse-graining steps can approach a <renormalization-group fixed point> before a relevant perturbation becomes large. Systems with different microscopic details but the same <renormalization-group fixed point> and relevant directions share universal critical behavior. For the quadratic model below, <Fourier modes> decouple, so eliminating a high-momentum shell produces a Gaussian <determinant> but does not couple the remaining modes. The anisotropic <gradient> kernel requires different length rescalings in the parallel and transverse directions. This is an <anisotropic renormalization group>, not an isotropic rescaling of all $d$ coordinates.

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose
$$
m(x_\parallel,\mathbf x_\perp)=\int\frac{dq_\parallel\,d^{d-1}q_\perp}{(2\pi)^d}\,
\widetilde m(q_\parallel,\mathbf q_\perp)e^{i(q_\parallel x_\parallel+\mathbf q_\perp\cdot\mathbf x_\perp)}.
$$
For a real scalar field $\widetilde m(-q)=\widetilde m(q)^*$. The parallel derivative becomes $iq_\parallel$, and the transverse <Laplacian> becomes $-q_\perp^2$. The latter is squared in the energy, producing $q_\perp^4$, not $q_\perp^2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\beta H=\frac12\int_q[Kq_\parallel^2+Lq_\perp^4+t]|\widetilde m(q)|^2-h\widetilde m(0).}
$$
The zero-momentum source is understood in a finite box or as a uniform-field distribution before taking the <thermodynamic limit>. With an orthonormal box transform the source is $-h\sqrt V\,m_0$. The positive coefficients and $t>0$ give a normalizable <Gaussian field theory>, with mean uniform field $\overline m=h/t$ and connected Fourier <covariance> $[Kq_\parallel^2+Lq_\perp^4+t]^{-1}$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $b,c>1$, rescale $q'_\parallel=bq_\parallel$, $\mathbf q'_\perp=c\mathbf q_\perp$, and set $B=bc^{d-1}$. Use the Fourier-amplitude convention
$$
\widetilde m'(q')=\widetilde m(q)/z.
$$
The momentum measure contributes $B^{-1}$, and substitution into the quadratic <statistical Hamiltonian> gives
$$
\boxed{K'=\frac{z^2}{Bb^2}K,\quad L'=\frac{z^2}{Bc^4}L,\quad
 t'=\frac{z^2}{B}t,\quad h'=zh.}
$$
In the uniform source the momentum delta function cancels the <Jacobian determinant> of the measure, explaining why $h'$ does not have an additional $B^{-1}$. In a full <momentum-shell renormalization group> transformation integrate out the fast modes first. Their uncoupled <Gaussian integrals> contribute an additive <free-energy density>; the displayed transformations describe the slow-mode <statistical Hamiltonian> after coordinate and field rescaling.

The field convention must be stated. In real space, $x'_\parallel=x_\parallel/b$ and $\mathbf x'_\perp=\mathbf x_\perp/c$, and Fourier inversion gives $m'(x')=Bm(x)/z$. Thus if instead the symbol $m'=m/z_x$ refers to real-space amplitudes, $z=Bz_x$. Then the same transformation reads $K'=Bz_x^2K/b^2$, $L'=Bz_x^2L/c^4$, $t'=Bz_x^2t$, $h'=Bz_xh$. These are equivalent normalizations, not different <critical exponents>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Keeping both <gradient> coefficients fixed requires $z^2=Bb^2=Bc^4$. Hence
$$
c=b^{1/2},\qquad B=b^{(d+1)/2},\qquad z=b^{(d+5)/4}.
$$
Equivalently the real-space field normalization is $z_x=z/B=b^{(3-d)/4}$. The transformed thermal and source couplings are
$$
\boxed{t'=b^2t,\qquad h'=b^{(d+5)/4}h,\qquad y_t=2,\quad y_h=(d+5)/4.}
$$
This is the <Gaussian fixed point> at $t=h=0$, with anisotropic effective volume exponent $D_{\rm eff}=(d+1)/2$. The stiffnesses remain positive parameters labeling the normalization of coordinates and fields. A strictly positive $t$ is a relevant perturbation away from the <renormalization-group fixed point>, not itself a critical <renormalization-group fixed point>.

The two <correlation lengths> also follow directly by balancing the kernel: $Kq_\parallel^2\sim t$ gives $\xi_\parallel\sim(K/t)^{1/2}$, while $Lq_\perp^4\sim t$ gives $\xi_\perp\sim(L/t)^{1/4}$. Their different exponents are consistent with the required anisotropic scaling. An omitted scalar quartic interaction has scaling <eigenvalue> $4-D_{\rm eff}=(7-d)/2$, so the quadratic <renormalization-group fixed point> alone does not establish the interacting smectic's critical behavior below physical dimension seven.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $f=-V^{-1}\log\mathcal Z$ be the dimensionless <free-energy density>. The transformed volume is $V'=V/B$. Including the normalization from eliminated modes gives
$$
f(t,h)=g_b(t,h)+B^{-1}f(t',h'),
$$
where $g_b$ is an additive shell contribution. After subtraction of regular backgrounds, the leading singular part obeys the <scaling hypothesis for critical phenomena>
$$
f_s(t,h)=b^{-D_{\rm eff}}f_s(b^2t,b^{(d+5)/4}h),\qquad D_{\rm eff}=(d+1)/2.
$$
Choose $b=t^{-1/2}$ for the high-temperature side $t>0$. Then the nominal homogeneous form is
$$
\boxed{f_s(t,h)=t^{(d+1)/4}g_f\!\left(h/t^{(d+5)/8}\right),\quad
\alpha=(7-d)/4,\quad\Delta=(d+5)/8.}
$$
The volume exponent is $D_{\rm eff}$, so inserting $d$ in the isotropic hyperscaling formula would give the wrong answer.

There are two qualifications to interpreting this as the total <free energy>. Completing the Gaussian square gives the source contribution exactly as $-h^2/(2t)$, and the zero-field <determinant> contributes
$$
f(t,h)=\frac12\int_q\log(Kq_\parallel^2+Lq_\perp^4+t)-\frac{h^2}{2t}+\text{normalization}.
$$
Rescaling $q_\parallel\sim t^{1/2}$ and $q_\perp\sim t^{1/4}$ gives the power $t^{(d+1)/4}$, while $2\Delta-1=(d+1)/4$ makes the source term consistent with it. Cutoff-dependent analytic terms need not obey the homogeneous law. Furthermore <Gaussian free-energy logarithms at integer singular powers> replace the pure zero-field power by $t^n\log t$ when $(d+1)/4=n$ is a positive integer, including $d=3,7$. This is the usual logarithmic qualification of the nominal exponents. The quadratic model also has no stable negative-$t$ phase unless a stabilizing interaction is added.

= 3
{parent=Paper 69}
