= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the interaction <matrix> as $\mathsf J$ to distinguish it from its scalar strength. The printed pair sum contains both orientations and has no factor $1/2$. For a <positive-definite matrix> $\mathsf J$, completion of the square gives the <Hubbard–Stratonovich transformation>
$$
\int_{\mathbb R^N}d^Nm\,e^{-m^T\mathsf J^{-1}m+2m^T\sigma}
=\pi^{N/2}\sqrt{\det\mathsf J}\,e^{\sigma^T\mathsf J\sigma},
$$
since $m^T\mathsf J^{-1}m-2m^T\sigma=(m-\mathsf J\sigma)^T\mathsf J^{-1}(m-\mathsf J\sigma)-\sigma^T\mathsf J\sigma$. For the exponential kernel with $J>0$ and $\kappa>0$, positivity follows from the positive Fourier symbol derived in part (b), or from its finite open-chain <covariance> <matrix>.

Insert this identity into the <Ising model> partition sum and interchange the finite spin sum with the convergent <Gaussian integral>. The spin variables now factor independently:
$$
\sum_{\sigma_i=\pm1}e^{(2m_i+h)\sigma_i}=2\cosh(2m_i+h).
$$
It follows that
$$
\boxed{\mathcal Z=C\int_{\mathbb R^N}\prod_i dm_i\,
\exp\left[-\sum_{ij}m_i[\mathsf J^{-1}]_{ij}m_j+\sum_i\log(2\cosh(2m_i+h))\right],}
$$
with $C=(\pi^N\det\mathsf J)^{-1/2}$ for the measure used here. The factor two in the spin-field coupling is required by the double pair sum; replacing it by one would decouple a <statistical Hamiltonian> with a different normalization. The diagonal $J_{ii}\sigma_i^2=J$ is a spin-independent energy. Including it as printed makes the positive Gaussian identity directly applicable; if diagonal self-couplings are removed they should first be restored as an irrelevant constant before this real auxiliary-field decoupling.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Work first on the infinite chain, or with a periodized exponential kernel. Put $\rho=e^{-\kappa}$ and use lattice Fourier momentum $q\in[-\pi,\pi]$. Summing the geometric series gives the <exponentially coupled Ising inverse kernel>:
$$
J(q)=J\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\rho^{|n|}e^{iqn}
=J\frac{1-\rho^2}{1-2\rho\cos q+\rho^2}
=\frac{J\sinh\kappa}{\cosh\kappa-\cos q}>0.
$$
Thus
$$
J^{-1}(q)=\frac{\cosh\kappa-\cos q}{J\sinh\kappa}
=\frac{\tanh(\kappa/2)}J+\frac{1-\cos q}{J\sinh\kappa}.
$$
Although the original kernel is long-ranged, its inverse contains only a constant and a cosine and therefore is nearest-neighbor. Fourier orthogonality gives
$$
\sum_{ij}m_i[\mathsf J^{-1}]_{ij}m_j
=\frac1{2J\sinh\kappa}\sum_j(m_j-m_{j+1})^2
+\frac{\tanh(\kappa/2)}J\sum_jm_j^2.
$$
Substitution into part (a) yields
$$
\boxed{\mathcal Z=C\int\prod_jdm_j\,e^{-\sum_j[(m_j-m_{j+1})^2/(2J\sinh\kappa)+U(m_j)]},\quad
U(m)=\frac{\tanh(\kappa/2)}Jm^2-\log[2\cosh(2m+h)].}
$$
This exact bulk identity does not require replacing $\sinh\kappa$ by $\kappa$; small $\kappa$ is used later to motivate a smooth continuum field.

For a finite open chain the inverse has off-diagonal entries $-\rho/[J(1-\rho^2)]$, interior diagonal entries $(1+\rho^2)/[J(1-\rho^2)]$ and endpoint diagonal entries $1/[J(1-\rho^2)]$. Accordingly the displayed homogeneous potential acquires the endpoint correction $\rho(m_1^2+m_N^2)/[J(1+\rho)]$. These boundary terms do not change the bulk thermodynamic conclusion. Stating infinite-chain or periodized <boundary conditions> is necessary if the translation-invariant expression is asserted exactly.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With lattice spacing set to one and a smoothly varying auxiliary field, the discrete action becomes
$$
S[m]=\int_0^{\mathcal L}dx\left[\frac M2\left(\frac{dm}{dx}\right)^2+U(m)\right],\qquad
M=\frac1{J\sinh\kappa}.
$$
This is precisely the <Euclidean path integral> of a quantum particle with $\hbar=1$, mass $M$ and potential $U$. With fixed endpoints it gives the transition amplitude $\langle m_f|e^{-\mathcal L\widehat H_Q}|m_i\rangle$, while periodic classical <boundary conditions> give its trace. The corresponding operator is
$$
\boxed{\widehat H_Q=-\frac{J\sinh\kappa}{2}\frac{d^2}{dm^2}+U(m),\qquad
\mathcal Z_{\rm periodic}\propto\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\mathcal L\widehat H_Q}.}
$$
This is <classical Ising chain as a quantum particle>. It is the continuum approximation to the exact positive transfer kernel, so its numerical spectrum is accurate when the relevant auxiliary configurations vary slowly. The transfer-kernel reasoning and the domain-wall argument below also support the qualitative conclusion without treating a continuum approximation as an exact finite-lattice identity.

At zero field write $a=\tanh(\kappa/2)/J>0$. Expansion gives
$$
U(m)=-\log2+(a-2)m^2+\frac43m^4+O(m^6).
$$
A double well exists when $a<2$; the stationary points satisfy $am=\tanh(2m)$. For $a\geq2$ there is a single minimum at zero, with the quadratic curvature marginal at equality. Thus a double well is the low-effective-temperature regime of the model, not an unconditional property for every $J$. The exact potential remains confining because $U(m)=am^2-2|m|+O(1)$ at large $|m|$.

Even when two wells exist, a quantum particle with finite mass and a finite barrier tunnels between them. Its one-dimensional confining <Schrödinger operator> has a unique, strictly positive ground-state wavefunction. At $h=0$ reflection <symmetry (physics)> makes that state even, so every odd observable has zero expectation. An antisymmetric first excited state is split from it by a positive gap. A spectral expansion of an odd-observable correlation has factors $e^{-x(E_n-E_0)}$; the smallest contributing gap therefore gives a finite classical <correlation length>. The original spin observable, after decoupling, is $\tanh(2m+h)$, which is odd at zero field. Hence
$$
\boxed{\langle\sigma\rangle=0\ \text{at }h=0\text{ for finite }J\text{ and fixed }\kappa>0,\quad
\langle\sigma_0\sigma_r\rangle\to0.}
$$
A deep well can give an exponentially small <quantum tunnelling> gap and an extremely long <correlation length>, which can mimic order in a finite sample, but it does not produce true long-range order.

The classical counterpart is the finite cost of a <domain wall>. Because the pair sum is doubled, a sharp wall has dimensionless energy
$$
E_{\rm wall}=4J\sum_{r\geq1}r e^{-\kappa r}=\frac{4Je^{-\kappa}}{(1-e^{-\kappa})^2}<\infty.
$$
At any finite temperature such walls have nonzero fugacity, divide a long chain into domains and destroy spontaneous magnetization. The range is large for $\kappa\ll1$ but finite. The zero-temperature limit and an infinite-range limit are singular and must not be interchanged casually with the <thermodynamic limit>; an infinite-range interaction also requires an extensive normalization before comparing models.

A nonzero $h$ tilts the potential: to first order $U_h(m)=U_0(m)-h\tanh(2m)+O(h^2)$. A positive field favors the positive well and produces positive magnetization. For fixed finite parameters the <ground state> and classical <free energy> vary smoothly, so there is no zero-field discontinuity due merely to a double-well bare potential. A discrete broken-symmetry state appears only in an appropriate singular limit, not by ignoring <quantum tunnelling>.

More generally a local $d$-dimensional classical statistical model can be sliced along one coordinate and represented by a transfer operator. That coordinate becomes imaginary time; the other $d-1$ coordinates become the spatial coordinates of a quantum system. Infinite extent in the transfer direction corresponds to its ground-state, or zero-quantum-temperature, limit. The classical <Ising model> is thus related to a quantum Ising system in $d-1$ spatial dimensions, with transverse dynamics generated by the transfer operator. For $d=1$ this is quantum mechanics of finitely many local variables, where <quantum tunnelling> restores <symmetry (physics)>. For $d\geq2$ the quantum system has infinitely many spatial degrees of freedom and can have distinct symmetry-broken <ground states>, allowing a classical finite-temperature ordering transition. Locality and anisotropy of the mapping determine the quantum couplings; the dimension correspondence alone does not fix them.
